Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Ganpatram of Amod and His Departure to Akshardham In Amod, the village of the eminent devotee Muktaraj Dinanath Bhatt, lived a pious Brahmin devotee named Vipra Ganpatram. Through constant association with holy sadhus, his satsang had matured profoundly, and he recognized the supreme glory of Shriji Maharaj. He firmly understood Shriji Maharaj to be none other than Supreme Lord Shri Purushottam Narayan Himself—the ultimate cause of all divine incarnations. Unceasingly chanting the holy name &ldquo;Swaminarayan Swaminarayan,&rdquo; he would gather the village devotees day and night to read and contemplate the sacred Vachanamrut. He harbored no taste for worldly gossip or idle village talk.
Vipra Ganpatram possessed immense divine love and reverence for Sadguru Gopalanand Swami and Sadguru Shukanand Swami. Once, observing his intense spiritual yearning (mumukshuta), Shriji Maharaj Himself remarked, &ldquo;The soul of this Ganpatram is truly like that of Muktanand Swami.&rdquo;
In his advanced age, Ganpatram once fell critically ill. At that time, he was blessed with the divine vision (darshan) of Shriji Maharaj along with all the great liberated souls and saints. Maharaj said to him, &ldquo;Ganpatram, from the wealth you have saved, fulfill whatever divine wish you hold—offer what you desire to the mandir, and give whatever you wish to your family members.&rdquo;
At that juncture, around sixty sadhus from Gadhpur and Junagadh were staying at the local mandir in Amod. Ganpatram invited all the sadhus as well as the village devotees to his home and declared, &ldquo;I have five thousand rupees in savings. Of this, one thousand rupees shall be offered to the local mandir here, one thousand rupees to Shri Lakshmi-Narayan Dev of Vadtal, one thousand rupees for my final rites and ceremonies, one thousand rupees for my daughters, and one thousand rupees for my wife. Tomorrow at noon, I shall depart from this mortal frame. Therefore, please arrange an early meal for all the revered saints.&rdquo;
The following day, the sadhus prepared the meal early, offered naivedya (thaal) to Thakorji, partook of the sacred meal, and then arrived at Ganpatram&rsquo;s residence. Beholding them, Ganpatram said with immense joy, &ldquo;I am beholding the divine darshan of Shriji Maharaj and all the great Muktas.&rdquo; Hearing this, Sadguru Mahapurushdas Swami, a revered yogi of Junagadh, said, &ldquo;Please convey our Jai Swaminarayan to Gopalanand Swami.&rdquo; Thereafter, the saints returned to the mandir, and at that very moment, Ganpatram peacefully shed his mortal body and ascended to Akshardham. Thus, having foretold his departure and beholding the divine presence of Shri Hari, he entered the eternal abode.
