Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Hirabhai Suhagiya of Pithvadi and Shriji Maharaj In the village of Pithvadi lived Hirabhai Suhagiya, a devout devotee who held unwavering faith in the supreme divinity of Shri Hari (paripurna nishtha). He always served according to the divine will and pleasure of Shriji Maharaj.
Once, Shriji Maharaj was leading the wedding procession (jaan) of Dada Khachar to the village of Bhatvadar, accompanied by many holy saints and devotees. On the way, the entire wedding party halted overnight at Pithvadi. On this auspicious occasion, Hirabhai Suhagiya devoutly performed the puja of Shriji Maharaj and placed five hundred koris (the local currency of that era) at the holy lotus feet of the Lord.
Testing his devotion and detachment, Shriji Maharaj said, &ldquo;We shall distribute this money during Dada Khachar&rsquo;s wedding among the bards, folk singers, stage performers, and mendicants (dhādhis, langhās, bhavāyās, and māngans).&rdquo; Upon hearing this, Hirabhai folded his hands and replied with a cheerful smile, &ldquo;O Krupanidhan (ocean of mercy)! You alone bestowed this wealth upon me, and I have merely offered it back to You! Please do with it whatever pleases You! When our soul arrives in Your divine abode (Akshardham), grant us whatever reward You deem fit!&rdquo;
Such was the sublime understanding and surrender of Hirabhai. Highlighting this divine episode, the sacred scripture Harililamrut (Kalash 8, Vishram 40) records: &ldquo;Pachhi Harji Patel sujāne, pujā kidhi Prabhuni te tāne..! Puri pānchse koriyo lāvi, Bhagavān-ne bhet dharāvi..! Kahe Krishna ā koriyo leshu, dhādhi langhāo-ne vahenchi deshu..! Bolyā Harji jodine hāth, karo jem game tem Nāth…! Hu to Akshardhām mozhār, enu fal āp pāse lenār…!&rdquo; (Meaning: The wise devotee Harji Patel worshipped the Lord and offered five hundred koris to Him. Shri Hari tested him saying He would distribute the coins to folk performers, but Harji humbly folded his hands and said, &lsquo;O Lord, do as You please; I shall receive its true fruit directly from You in Akshardham!&rsquo;)
Later, the Satsangis migrated from Gorviyali village and settled in Pithvadi, nearly 90 kilometers away. The local Darbar (ruler) of Pithvadi village generously granted these Patidar farmers as much agricultural land as they requested. Through their hard work and agricultural skill, these devoted farmers brought great prosperity to Pithvadi. With time, their sons came of age and were married within Pithvadi itself. Eventually, the ancestral farmland had to be partitioned between the two sons of Hirabhai—Punjabhai and Jadavbhai.
Of the land gifted to Hirabhai by the Darbar, half was exceptionally fertile, while the remaining half was rocky wasteland (kharābo). When the partition was negotiated between the two brothers, the elder son Punjabhai&rsquo;s family shrewdly insisted on dividing the land so that all the fertile soil fell into their share, leaving mostly wasteland for the other share. Despite repeated mediation, no amicable agreement could be reached. Since the in-laws of both brothers were prominent members of the local Satsang community, this domestic rift began causing unrest and disturbance (vikshep) among the devotees of Pithvadi.
Seeking a divine resolution to this discord, Bhagabhai and Mulabhai traveled to Gadhada to seek the guidance of Bhagwan Shree Swaminarayan. They explained the prevailing disharmony in Pithvadi and prayed, &ldquo;Maharaj, please grace Pithvadi with Your presence and resolve this discord among the devotees.&rdquo;
Shriji Maharaj responded, &ldquo;At this time, We will not be able to visit.&rdquo; Saying so, Maharaj stood up, placed both His holy lotus hands upon the heads of Mulabhai and Bhagabhai, and blessed them: &ldquo;Go! By the divine spiritual power of your celibacy (brahmacharya), whatever you decide, We shall stand firmly with you. Go and resolve the matter yourselves.&rdquo;
Carrying Shriji Maharaj&rsquo;s divine mandate, Bhagabhai and Mulabhai returned to Pithvadi, assembled all the devotees, and endeavored to partition Hirabhai&rsquo;s land equitably. However, Punjabhai&rsquo;s family remained unyielding and refused to compromise. Seeing this, Bhagabhai and Mulabhai stood up, placed their hands upon Jadavbhai&rsquo;s head just as Maharaj had done to them in Gadhada, and declared: &ldquo;Brother Jadavbhai! Accept this wasteland share with an open heart. We assure you on the solemn word of Maharaj that even though this land appears barren today, your family shall blossom with golden flowers from it! Therefore, accept it gladly.&rdquo;
Jadavbhai joyfully obeyed the divine directive conveyed by Bhagabhai and Mulabhai. The partition was officially recorded in the state registers of the Gaekwad government, and those historical land records remain preserved in the government archives to this day.
This sacred account was personally recounted by their descendant, Shivlalbhai. Owing to the divine blessings of Shri Hari upon this noble lineage, Hirabapa&rsquo;s descendants remain steadfast and deeply rooted in Satsang to this day. Among his descendants, prominent saints of Shree Swaminarayan Gurukul such as Sadguru Purani Shri Balkrishnadasji Swami and Akshar-nivasi Shri Haridasji Swami have served as glorious ornaments of the Satsang fellowship. Shivlalbhai&rsquo;s son Hasmukhbhai and grandson Vasudevbhai are both proud alumni of Rajkot Gurukul and maintain deep devotion to Satsang.
In the sacred text Bhaktachintamani (Chapter 114, verses 40–43), Sadguru Shri Nishkulanand Swami has commemorated the pure and loving devotees of Pithvadi: &ldquo;Dhanya dhanya Pithvadi gām, jiyā bhakta vase nishkām..! Āmbo Megho Hiro Bhāyo nām, Rudo Punjo Hirji Jerām..! Rājo Bhago Mulo Jetho jan, Vālo Kalo Premji pāvan..! Hiru Lādu Velu Kunvarbāi, motā mukta e kanbimāi..! Dvij Jivone Putalibāi, sut Shivo Govardhanbhāi..! Dvij Mono Rāghav Dayārām, Koli bhakta ek Bhojo nām..! Jan Jansāli Kalyān lai, bhakta chhe Pithvadiye kai..!&rdquo;
From the remembrance of the liberated souls in Shri Bhaktachintamani&hellip; 🙏
