Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Pratapsinh Darbar of Chhatrasa and Gunatitanand Swami In the Dhoraji taluka lies a small village named Chhatrasa. One night, the chieftain (darbar) of the village, Pratapsinh Darbar, was resting on a cot in the veranda (osari) of his residence. Past midnight, in a vivid dream, terrifying messengers of death (Yamadoots) appeared before him. In moments, he felt as though they were violently dragging him off his cot. Suddenly, he beheld a large gateway (deli), beyond which stood a magnificent mandir with sacred flags fluttering atop its high spires. Below in the courtyard, seated beside a pillar in the distance, a divine, luminous personality shouted authoritatively: “Hey you&hellip;! Stay away! He is our soul!” The moment He spoke, the messengers of death bowed reverently to this divine personality and retreated.
Pratapsinh Darbar awakened in shock and sat bolt upright on his bed. Sitting upon his cot, he pondered deeply: &ldquo;Those Yamadoots were indeed real, but who could that divine personality be who drove them away with a single command? If only I could meet Him in person, He would liberate me from the endless cycle of births and deaths and free me forever from the dread of Yamadoots.&rdquo;
The next morning, he narrated the entire experience to his uncle. His uncle was also a noble, spiritually inclined soul. He said, &ldquo;In this sacred land of Bharat, such great, enlightened souls wander in the garb of ascetic mendicants. Come, let us embark on a pilgrimage. Wandering through holy pilgrim places, we will surely find Him somewhere.&rdquo; With this thought, Pratapsinh and his uncle set out on a spiritual quest.
They visited holy pilgrimage sites including Dwarka, Somnath, and Prabhas Patan, constantly searching for the magnificent mandir and the divine personage seen in the dream. Wandering in their quest, they eventually reached Junagadh at the foothills of Mount Girnar. They searched all around the Bhavnath foothills and visited numerous mandirs, yet nowhere did they witness anything resembling the vision from the dream.
Finally, Pratapsinh grew disheartened and remarked to his uncle, &ldquo;Uncle, will I never be blessed with the darshan of that divine personality and that mandir?&rdquo; His uncle replied, &ldquo;At the foothills of Girnar, many seekers have found God in the guise of sants. Let us ask around!&rdquo; As they were conversing, a local resident said to them, &ldquo;Here in our Nagarwada area, a new mandir of Bhagwan Shree Swaminarayan has recently been built. It features a grand gateway and towering spires. Go and take darshan there; perhaps your heart’s desire may be fulfilled.&rdquo;
Upon hearing this, Pratapsinh and his uncle walked swiftly to the mandir in Nagarwada. Entering the temple precincts, upon seeing the grand gateway and the magnificent mandir of Shri Radharaman Dev, Darbar Pratapsinh exclaimed joyfully, &ldquo;Uncle! These are the very spires and this is the very gateway that I saw in my dream! Now, if only I can have the darshan of that great holy personage who commanded the Yamadoots to flee, my life will be eternally redeemed!&rdquo;
Both entered, took darshan of the deities, and walked toward the assembly hall (sabhamandap). There, leaning against a pillar, sat Sadguru Gunatitanand Swami. The moment he laid eyes upon Swami, the Darbar rushed forward and clasped Swami’s holy feet. Tears of joy streamed from his eyes. Sadguru Gunatitanand Swami smiled and warmly welcomed him: &ldquo;Come, Pratapsinh Darbar, welcome! Did those Yamadoots appear very terrifying to you?&rdquo;
With folded hands and a choked voice, Pratapsinh prayed, &ldquo;You delivered me from the clutches of the Yamadoots in my dream. You have showered immense grace upon me! I wandered in search of You for so long, and today my spiritual merits have borne fruit. Please take me under the shelter of Your holy feet and deliver me from the fear of Yamadoots and the bonds of worldly existence.&rdquo;
Swami smiled gently and said, &ldquo;Darbar! The worship (bhajan) of Bhagwan and His shelter alone can deliver a soul from the Yamadoots. Otherwise, no matter how much a mortal struggles, nothing avails at the final hour. In the end, vast estates, royal kingdoms, immense wealth, mansions, and orchards will all turn to mere dust. Therefore, seek refuge in the manifest Bhagwan Swaminarayan, wear the sacred kanthi, and in every action—walking, talking, eating, or drinking—chant the holy name &lsquo;Swaminarayan&hellip; Swaminarayan!&rsquo; At your final hour, Shri Hari Himself will arrive with a divine celestial chariot (viman) and escort you to Akshardham.&rdquo;
Swami further revealed, &ldquo;Once, while we were traveling on a spiritual tour (vicharan), we were extremely hungry. At that time, you offered us a millet loaf (rotlo) with great affection. That humble offering created an eternal bond of grace. For that act of kindness, we came that night to rescue you from the Yamadoots. It is the divine wish and commandment of our Ishtadev Shri Hari that if anyone offers even a piece of bread to a sadhu, we must ensure the supreme spiritual welfare of that soul.&rdquo;
Pratapsinh Bapu immediately received the sacred vows (panch vartaman) from Gunatitanand Swami and embraced Satsang. He spent the remainder of his life engaged in continuous devotion and remembrance of Shri Hari, ultimately attaining the divine abode of Akshardham.
