Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Nanabhai Vipra of Bhayavadar and His Devout Conviction in Satsang While travelling through the Satsang fellowship accompanied by sadhus and devotees, Shri Hari once arrived in Jamkandorna. Haribhaktas from the neighbouring village of Bhayavadar came to have Shri Hari&rsquo;s sacred darshan. They earnestly prayed to Shri Hari to grace Bhayavadar with His presence, to which Shri Hari graciously agreed, promising to visit in two to four days.
In Bhayavadar lived Nanabhai Vipra (a Brahmin) and his devout mother, Lilaben (Lilaba), who were staunch devotees. Lilaben had nurtured Nanabhai since childhood with the sublime values of Satsang and provided him with a good education. At that time, Nanabhai served as the pasayata (village executive guard and officer of law and order) for both Bhayavadar and Upleta villages. Rendering justice fearlessly and impartially without yielding to pressure or favour, he performed his duties with resolute valour. Being an ardent Satsangi, he observed the Panch Vartman (the five cardinal moral vows of Satsang) with utmost purity and discipline.
When the hostile Brahmins of the village learned that Shri Hari was soon to arrive in Bhayavadar, they burned with envy and resentment. Approaching Junabhai and other devotees, they vented their anger and threatened, &ldquo;Do not invite your Guru Swaminarayan to this village, nor provide Him accommodation! If you do, you will invite our bitter enmity and ultimately suffer the consequences. Therefore, heed our counsel and abandon Satsang!&rdquo;
The village devotees were thrown into a dilemma, wondering what to do: whether to endure the village&rsquo;s hostility or renounce Satsang. At that moment, Nanabhai&rsquo;s mother, Lilaba, spoke up with great courage: &ldquo;Our Satsang is treasured at the cost of our very heads! Do not worry in the least. Shriji Maharaj&rsquo;s stay shall be hosted right at my home; I fear no one.&rdquo;
As soon as Nanabhai Vipra learned of the threats, he rushed to the scene. Confronting the hostile Brahmins with stern eyes, he proclaimed resolutely: &ldquo;Bhagwan Swaminarayan is the beloved Ishtadev of my mother and myself! If anyone dares insult Him, you will face dire consequences! As the village pasayata, I have the authority to mete out the strictest punishment. Shri Hari shall reside at my home, and on that day, I will patrol armed with weapons. If anyone utters an insolent word, be prepared to face retribution! Even otherwise, no one in this village has any right to disrespect visiting sadhus or saints!&rdquo;
Witnessing the fearless courage, devotion, and firm conviction of Nanabhai Vipra and his mother Lilaba, the adversaries lost their nerve and dared not raise any opposition. When Shri Hari arrived in Bhayavadar, Lilaba and her son Nanabhai welcomed Him into their home amidst grand musical festivities, devotion, and reverence.
