Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Jiva Joshi of Jetpur and His Steadfast Faith in Satsang Jiva Joshi of Jetpur was a deeply faithful devotee who harbored boundless love and surrender towards Shri Hari. Despite facing fierce opposition, envy, and social hostility from his fellow caste Brahmins in Jetpur, he adhered to the principles of Satsang with unwavering resolve, for which the community subjected him to immense hardship. He earned a meager livelihood by performing small-scale priestly rituals (gorpadu) in the village. Observing Jiva Joshi&rsquo;s dire financial constraints, the compassionate, devotee-loving Shriji Maharaj had once personally borrowed four hundred rupees from the town to celebrate the sacred thread ceremony (yagnopavit) of Jiva Joshi&rsquo;s son with great pomp and honor.
Jiva Joshi strictly observed the five moral vows (panch vartaman) and abstained from consuming onions, garlic, and other forbidden foods. His fellow community Brahmins deeply resented his strict discipline. Once, on a festive occasion at the royal palace of the local ruler (Darbar) of Jetpur, a community feast was arranged for the Brahmins. The jealous Brahmins seized this opportunity to mislead the ruler. Approaching the Darbar, they declared, &ldquo;O Darbar! Today, our entire Brahmin community will dine at your royal feast, but one particular Brahmin among us will refuse to eat!&rdquo; Gullible and easily swayed, the Darbar immediately summoned Jiva Joshi and reprimanded him, &ldquo;Jiva, what kind of discord are you creating that you refuse to dine at anyone&rsquo;s home?&rdquo; Jiva Joshi humbly explained the code of righteous spiritual conduct and devotional disciplines of Satsang, but the ruler refused to listen. Jiva Joshi politely submitted, &ldquo;O Darbar! Let everyone else dine joyfully. What difference does it make if a single individual like me does not partake? I am bound by the sacred disciplines laid down by my Ishtadev, so I cannot consume food prepared with onions and garlic. The divine command of my Ishtadev is dearer to me than my very life (seer-saate).&rdquo;
Hearing this, the ruler was incensed that Jiva Joshi had defied his royal bidding. Enraged, he decreed, &ldquo;If you are unwilling to sit and dine with your caste brethren, then pack your belongings and leave Jetpur this instant!&rdquo;
Though initially perturbed, Jiva Joshi contemplated in his heart: &ldquo;Shriji Maharaj is all-merciful. Everything unfolds solely by His divine will. He who provides for all must surely have arranged my bread somewhere.&rdquo; Bearing firm faith, he gathered his son and daughter-in-law that very day, abandoned Jetpur, and traveled straight to Junagadh, where Shriji Maharaj was gracing the town at that time.
Arriving in Junagadh, he offered prostrations (dandavat pranam) at the holy feet of Shriji Maharaj and narrated the entire episode. Hearing how he remained steadfast, Maharaj rose from His cot and embraced him warmly with immense affection, exclaiming, &ldquo;Even when the ruler expelled you from your native home for upholding the sacred commands of Satsang, you did not waver! We are immensely pleased with your unshakable devotion.&rdquo;
Shriji Maharaj immediately instructed Shukmuni to write a letter addressed to the Patel brothers, Bhaga and Mulha, of Pithvadi village, recommending that they arrange proper residence and a reliable livelihood for Jiva Joshi. The compassionate Shri Hari specifically noted in the letter, &ldquo;This devotee is extraordinarily faithful and dear to Us. Realizing his greatness, take utmost care of him and attend to his needs with love and respect.&rdquo;
Carrying Shri Hari&rsquo;s sanctified letter, Jiva Joshi arrived in Pithvadi with his family, met the brothers Bhaga and Mulha, and handed them the letter. Both brothers held boundless devotion for Shri Hari. They immediately vacated a comfortable house for Bhaktaraj Jiva Joshi to reside in, provided all necessary household goods, and supplied essential provisions to his son and daughter-in-law, joyfully appointing him as the village priest.
From Adarsh Bhaktagatha&hellip; 🙏
