Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Mava Bhagat of Thanagalol and His Resolute Conviction in Satsang In the village of Thanagalol, there lived a devoted Haribhakt named Jasa Bhagat. Visiting sadhus always found a welcoming shelter and hospitable lodging at his home during their spiritual travels (vicharan). On one occasion, Sadguru Muktanand Swami arrived in Thanagalol accompanied by his assembly of saints. In the afternoon, Muktanand Swami, the saints, and Jasa Bhagat set out toward the river to bathe. On the way, they observed a spiritual seeker circumambulating a sacred pipal tree, continuously chanting &ldquo;Ram, Ram&rdquo; with uninterrupted devotion.
Sadguru Muktanand Swami paused upon seeing him and inquired of Jasa Bhagat, &ldquo;He appears to be a genuine spiritual seeker (mumukshu). Who is this devotee?&rdquo; Jasa Bhagat replied, &ldquo;Swami, he is Mava Bhagat of our village. He is remarkably innocent, noble, and an unswerving devotee of God.&rdquo;
Muktanand Swami requested, &ldquo;Please call him over.&rdquo; Jasa Bhagat called out to him, and Mava Bhagat approached with folded hands, reverently offering his prostrations (&ldquo;Narayan!&rdquo;). Sitting beneath the shade of a nearby tree, Muktanand Swami spoke of the supreme glory of God. Absorbing Swami’s nectar-like words, Mava Bhagat became completely immersed in divine spiritual bliss. Swami then asked him, &ldquo;Mava Bhagat, we are going to the river to bathe; will you come with us?&rdquo; Mava Bhagat gladly accompanied them.
As the saints walked along the village path, they chanted the divine dhun, &ldquo;Swaminarayan&hellip; Swaminarayan&hellip;&rdquo;. Mava Bhagat was so profoundly absorbed in chanting the name of Lord Ram that he harmoniously merged the two chants with innocent fervor: &ldquo;Ram&hellip; Ram&hellip;, Swami&hellip; Ram&hellip; Swami&hellip; Ram&hellip; Narayan&hellip;&rdquo;. Reaching the river, the saints bathed and then sat upon the soft sands of the riverbed, engaging in elevated spiritual discourses. Sadguru Muktanand Swami revealed to Mava Bhagat the supreme glory of manifest (pragat) Shriji Maharaj, the divine cause of all incarnations (Avataron na Avatari). Deeply moved by profound faith, Mava Bhagat embraced the satsang vows and became a dedicated devotee.
Mava Bhagat prayed to Swami, &ldquo;Please bless me with the divine darshan of this manifest Supreme Lord!&rdquo;
A few days later, as Muktanand Swami set out from Thanagalol toward Junagadh, he brought Mava Bhagat along so that he could have darshan of Shri Hari. Upon reaching Junagadh, they learned that Shriji Maharaj was currently residing in Meghpur, so Swami proceeded to Meghpur.
At that very moment, a violent clash had erupted with local bards (Bhats), during which Shri Hari&rsquo;s faithful attendant (parshad), Mulji Bhagat, was martyred and attained Akshardham. When the Kathi Darbars saw that Muktanand Swami had brought a newly introduced spiritual seeker into the midst of such unrest, they immediately expressed concern to Shriji Maharaj, saying, &ldquo;O Maharaj! We Kathis are accustomed to such strife and have no difficulty, but witnessing this turmoil and conflict, this simple Kanbi farmer will stumble! It will harm his spiritual journey, and he will lose all conviction in Your supreme divinity!&rdquo;
Looking at the pious seeker from past lives, Mava Bhagat, Shriji Maharaj firmly replied, &ldquo;He is no ordinary Satsangi! His conviction is steadfast and unbreakable. He has been initiated into Satsang by Muktanand Swami; therefore, under no circumstances can any doubt ever enter his heart!&rdquo;
Mava Bhagat had the divine darshan of Shriji Maharaj, stayed for a short time, and then returned peacefully to his village of Thanagalol without a single doubt in his heart. Seeing his unwavering faith, all the Kathi Darbars were filled with profound astonishment and reverence.
