Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Sadguru Nrusinhanand Swami and Lakhu Kandoi Approximately eighteen kilometers from Manavadar lies the village of Zinzari. While touring for the propagation of Satsang (satsang vicharan), Sadguru Nrusinhanand Swami arrived in Zinzari and lodged at the village chora (public meeting veranda). After doing darshan of Shri Ramchandra Mahaprabhu at the village shrine, he sat quietly in a solitary corner absorbed in deep meditation on Shriji Maharaj. At that time, the Diwan (chief minister) and the Kamdar (state administrator) of Manavadar arrived there on official government business and noticed this ascetic yogi in profound meditation. Being spiritually inclined seekers (mumukshus), they performed darshan of Shri Ramchandraji and sat reverently beside Swami. The moment they sat near him, their hearts experienced an overwhelming spiritual serenity and an extraordinary divine feeling.
After some time, Swami opened his eyes. Greeting him with &ldquo;Ram Ram,&rdquo; they inquired, &ldquo;O Mahatmaji! Who are you? Who is your Guru, and which sampradaya do you belong to?&rdquo; Swami replied in his naturally detached ascetic manner, &ldquo;We are wandering mendicants (ramta ram)—here today, somewhere else tomorrow. The world knows this physical body as Nrusinhanand. My Guru is the manifest Supreme Lord, Bhagwan Shri Swaminarayan Himself, and by His divine command, we tour from village to village spreading Satsang.&rdquo;
The moment they heard the sacred name of Swaminarayan Mahaprabhu from Swami&rsquo;s lips, both officials instantly entered a divine state of samadhi! In that transcendental vision, they beheld Shri Hari seated upon a divine golden throne in Akshardham, surrounded by liberated souls (Akshar-muktas), prominent deities, and Ishwars singing His praises. When they awakened from samadhi, firm and unshakeable faith in Swaminarayan Mahaprabhu was established in their hearts. Prostrating at Swami’s lotus feet, they accepted the moral vows (vartman) and became devoted satsangis.
Later, while Nrusinhanand Swami resided in Gadhpur, he made it a daily practice to order a quarter seer (pasher) of ganthiya and pendas from Lakhu Kandoi, a local sweetmaker. Every day, Swami would offer this food to Shri Hari as thal, and distribute the sanctified prasadi to visiting ascetic sadhus and devotees; he never consumed any of it himself. One day, his disciple, Sadhu Narayanvallabhdas, asked, &ldquo;Gurudev, you order these sweets and snacks every day, yet you never eat them yourself and give them away to others. Poor Lakhu Kandoi is destitute; how long will he be able to bear this financial strain?&rdquo;
Sadguru Nrusinhanand Swami smiled and revealed, &ldquo;Narayanvallabhdas, I do not wish for Lakhu Kandoi to remain poor! By ordering his pendas and ganthiya daily and offering them to Maharaj, I am eradicating his poverty. For this reason, I do not eat them myself; rather, by feeding this holy prasadi to renounced sadhus and noble devotees, I am severing the roots of his misfortune and destitution.&rdquo;
Within a short span of time, Lakhu Kandoi flourished and became a wealthy, respected merchant, well-known throughout the entire Sampradaya. Even today, his descendants reside in the Kandivali area of Mumbai, living devoted and faithful satsangi lives. His family members lovingly recount that Sadguru Nrusinhanand Swami had bestowed a divine blessing upon them: &ldquo;Prosperity and happiness shall remain in your lineage for seven generations.&rdquo; The ways of great saints are truly unique—they do not indulge themselves, yet by nourishing others, they bring boundless prosperity and spiritual joy to souls. It is solely through the blessings of Nrusinhanand Swami that the family continues to thrive in happiness and peace.
From &lsquo;Sant Aakhyan&rsquo;&hellip; 🙏
