Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Sadguru Nityanand Swami and the Triumph in Scriptural Debate On the auspicious day of Ashadh Sud 1, Samvat 1860, Shriji Maharaj arrived in the village of Meghpur from Piplana. He presided over an assembly held at the home of devotee Narayanbhai Soni, a pious disciple of Sadguru Ramanand Swami. In that assembly, Shri Hari delivered profound discourses on the frailty and transience of the mortal human body, emphasizing that utilizing this physical frame of five elements to worship Bhagwan and attain ultimate liberation (moksha) is the sole worthy purpose of human life.
Seated in that assembly was Dinmani Sharma, an erudite scholar originally from Uttar Pradesh, who had previously had Maharaj&rsquo;s darshan in Unjha. At his very first sight of Shriji Maharaj&rsquo;s divine teeth and radiant countenance in Unjha, he had resolved never to return to this perilous and binding worldly existence. Today, in the assembly of Meghpur, his firm spiritual determination culminated in a heartfelt prayer. He humbly pleaded, &ldquo;O Maharaj! Please initiate me as a sadhu.&rdquo; Witnessing his intense yearning to receive Bhagavata Diksha, Shri Hari gently inquired to test his resolve, &ldquo;Dinmani Sharma, do you not wish to return home?&rdquo; Dinmani Sharma replied with unwavering conviction, &ldquo;O Maharaj! From the very moment I had darshan of Your holy lotus feet in Unjha, my home, family, and all worldly attachments have been incinerated within my heart, and I have no desire to revive them ever again.&rdquo;
The following day, Shriji Maharaj formally initiated him into the sant order according to Vedic rites, bestowing upon him the name &lsquo;Nityanand Swami.&rsquo; Shri Hari prophetically declared, &ldquo;In erudition and scholarship, he will be peerless across the entire Uddhava Sampradaya and shall achieve supreme victory (digvijay) in scriptural debates.&rdquo;
As time progressed, listening to the divine discourses of Sadguru Nityanand Swami, Sadguru Muktanand Swami—revered as the caring mother of the Satsang fellowship—would often exclaim with awe: &ldquo;No one can articulate the true inner intent of Bhagwan Ved Vyas as masterfully as Nityanand Muni. When Nitya Muni expounds upon the sacred text of Srimad Bhagavatam, he reveals the profound majesty and glory of Purushottam Narayan just as Uddhavaji understood Him.&rdquo;
Whenever Sadguru Nityanand Swami sat upon the discourse seat and elucidated subtle and esoteric philosophical truths, Shri Hari Himself would repeatedly exclaim in divine ecstasy, &ldquo;Hare! Hare!&rdquo; Shriji Maharaj frequently observed that Nitya Muni possessed profound, unfathomable wisdom comparable to Brahmaji. Maharaj repeatedly granted him the sanctified food (prasadi) from His own dining plate (thaal) and honored him by draping precious prasadi shawls around his shoulders. Nitya Muni possessed boundless virtues. Despite being a paramount scholar and an unmatched dialectician, he always conducted himself with deep humility, strictly following the guidance and noble wishes of Yogiraj Sadguru Gopalanand Swami.
When Shri Hari visited Loya-Nagadka at the earnest insistence of Sura Bapu and stayed there for a month and a half, the divine lilas of Shakotsav were celebrated daily, with pilgrim congregations arriving from numerous villages. During that auspicious festival as well, Sadguru Nityanand Swami presided over the Vyasapitha each day to narrate the Fifth Canto of Srimad Bhagavatam. Shri Hari remarked that the narrative of King Bharat in this Fifth Canto is deeply miraculous and instructive: Bharatji renounced his vast kingdom through fierce detachment, yet lacking extraordinary, singular love and devotion directed exclusively towards Bhagwan, he became emotionally attached to a fawn and consequently had to undergo three rebirths.
On another occasion, Shriji Maharaj visited Junagadh at the formal invitation of the Nawab. The local Nagar scholars petitioned the Nawab to arrange a philosophical debate with Swaminarayan. Shriji Maharaj serenely said, &ldquo;All your queries and doubts will be thoroughly answered by our Nityanand Muni.&rdquo; The Nagar pundits questioned haughtily, &ldquo;Have the scholars of Vadodara, Kashi, or Dakor established Swaminarayan as Bhagwan, or do you merely claim divinity on your own?&rdquo; The calm, poised, and profound Nitya Muni responded, &ldquo;When the sun rises, darkness is dispelled spontaneously. The sun remains the sun whether someone chooses to believe in it or not. In the exact same manner, whether you accept it or not, the entire creation reveres Him as Bhagwan; your personal denials make no difference whatsoever.&rdquo;
Then a scholar named Narsinh Pandya raised an objection based on scripture, &ldquo;Why do the scriptures state, &lsquo;Vande mahapurusha te charanaravindam&rsquo; (I bow to the lotus feet of the Mahapurusha)?&rdquo; Swami promptly replied, &ldquo;The scriptures proclaim that the true fortune of a jiva lies in becoming the dust of the Lord&rsquo;s holy feet; one cannot sit upon His divine crown.&rdquo; Narsinh Pandya stubbornly argued, &ldquo;That implies only the lotus feet of the Mahapurusha are holy, while the rest of His body is not.&rdquo; Hearing this absurd reasoning, the Nawab himself intervened, exclaiming, &ldquo;Pandya, you have completely lost your senses! The entire divine being of Khuda (God) is holy and pure (pak)! No one can ever claim any part of God to be unholy (napak)!&rdquo;
Thus, after extensive debates and flawless scriptural reasoning, Swami triumphed completely in that assembly as well.
