Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Sachchidanand Swami and the Vairagis of Mandavdhar One afternoon in Gadhpur, Shri Hari was seated in an assembly beneath the neem tree (Limbataru). Sadguru Sachchidanand Swami, the very embodiment of love, arrived from Lakshmi Vadi carrying fragrant flower garlands. Prostrating reverently before Shriji Maharaj with dandvat pranam, Swami adorned Him with floral garlands, a decorative flower cap (topi), and floral armlets (bajubandh), performing His devotional puja. Shriji Maharaj was immensely pleased with Swami.
At that moment, devotees from Mandavdhar village arrived. Having divine darshan of Shri Hari, they humbly pleaded, &ldquo;O Maharaj! Presently, our jaggery crushing mills (chinchoda) are operating in the farms. A troupe of eighty obstinate Vairagi Bawas has descended upon our village. They are causing great havoc, demanding one large cauldron (kadai) of jaggery per Bawa—eighty cauldrons in total! They threaten to halt our mills through occult spells (mantravidya) if we refuse. In this manner, they harass the villagers relentlessly day after day. Please shower Your divine grace so that the villagers find relief and our entire year&rsquo;s labor and harvest are not ruined.&rdquo;
Bhaktavatsal Shriji Maharaj smiled and said, &ldquo;O devotees! Our Sachchidanand Swami here has the perfect remedy for this troupe of Vairagis. Swami, please accompany these devotees and protect them from the menace of these Bawas.&rdquo;
Obeying Shri Hari&rsquo;s divine command, Sadguru Sachchidanand Swami traveled from Gadhada to Mandavdhar along with the devotees. Seeing Swami accompanying the villagers, the Vairagis became even more infuriated. They began boasting loudly before Swami. One of the Vairagis arrogantly bragged, &ldquo;I possess such yogic powers that I can sit comfortably inside this boiling hot cauldron of jaggery!&rdquo; Swami calmly replied, &ldquo;You may only be able to sit in a hot cauldron, but I can sit directly in the blazing fire beneath it without being burned in the slightest; such is the supreme power bestowed upon me by my Ishtadev, Bhagwan Shri Swaminarayan!&rdquo;
Swami then challenged them, &ldquo;Let us set a condition: if you can accomplish what I am about to do, only then will I accept that you are truly accomplished in yogic sciences.&rdquo; Saying this, Swami unhitched the bullocks from the crushing mill, tied a single raw, unspun cotton thread around his waist, and fastened the other end to the heavy mill. Then, walking gracefully in circles, Swami operated and rotated the massive jaggery crusher solely with that delicate cotton thread! After turning the mill for a long time, Swami addressed the Vairagi Bawas, &ldquo;Now, whoever among you can rotate this mill with a single strand of raw cotton thread may take all the jaggery.&rdquo;
Witnessing this astonishing yogic feat, the Vairagis were struck with awe and defeat, and fled the village muttering amongst themselves. Thus, Swami safely protected the villagers from the nuisance of the Khakhi Vairagis.
On another occasion, heavy rains lashed Gadhpur, filling the Radhavav stepwell to the brim with crystal-clear water. Sachchidanand Swami prayed to Maharaj, &ldquo;O Maharaj! Lord Varuna is pleased and Radhavav is overflowing with water; please come to bathe and enjoy water sports along with the holy sadhus and devotees.&rdquo;
Accompanied by all the sadhus, Shri Hari proceeded from the Darbargadh to Radhavav amidst joyous music and ecstatic devotional kirtans. Those who knew how to swim leapt into the stepwell to bathe. The attendants (parshads) engaged in playful water games (patudi-dav). Shri Hari then called out, &ldquo;If there is any mighty swimmer capable of reaching the very bottom of this deep stepwell to measure its depth, let him step forward.&rdquo; When no one volunteered, Shri Hari Himself stood on the steps, tied a loincloth (langot), and dove deep into the water to reach the bed of the well. After a considerable time beneath the surface, He emerged carrying a radiant crystal gem (sphatikmani stone). Handing it to Dada Khachar, He said, &ldquo;Give this sphatikmani to Laduba.&rdquo; Shri Hari had previously taught Dada Khachar how to swim; seeing Dada Khachar floating effortlessly on his back in the padmasana (lotus) posture, Shri Hari was thoroughly delighted.
Shri Hari then sat under a tamarind tree and commanded Nityanand Swami. Swami climbed a nearby peepal tree, took a deep dive into the stepwell, reached the bottom, and remained immersed in meditation for half an hour before resurfacing. Maharaj was exceptionally pleased; He gifted Swami a precious pair of white shawls worth two thousand rupees, which He had been wearing, and placed both His holy hands on Swami&rsquo;s head in benediction. After bathing together for a long time, Shri Hari, the sadhus, and the devotees returned to the Darbargadh.
On the mound near this stepwell, honoring the sacred site of Maharaj&rsquo;s maha-prasadi, Acharya Shri Viharilalji Maharaj later constructed a commemorative chhatri (memorial dome).
